"This unit has already described two layers of the meridian network: the twelve main meridians, each tied to a specific organ, and the eight extraordinary vessels, acting as reservoirs for the whole system. Chinese medicine describes a third, finer layer sitting between them: fifteen connecting channels, short branches that split off from each of the twelve main meridians plus three additional ones, specifically to link each meridian more directly to its interior-exterior partner covered in an earlier lesson. The purpose of this extra layer follows directly from a gap in the two already covered. The twelve main meridians run their own separate courses, and while their paired relationship — the interior-exterior pairing discussed earlier — is well established in theory, the two paired meridians don't automatically touch along their main paths. The connecting channels are the short, direct bridges that actually link a meridian to its partner, making the paired relationship a physical connection rather than only a conceptual one. Practically, this finer layer explains cases where a point on one meridian produces an effect specifically on its paired partner, rather than somewhere else along its own path — a more targeted version of the general referred-pain logic covered earlier in this unit. Adding this layer doesn't change anything about the overall picture; it fills in a gap the two-layer system left open, the way a rough sketch of a city's main roads still needs the smaller connecting streets before the map is genuinely complete.": "5b1b44b8fa05",
